Orthonym — Third-Party Components
=================================

Orthonym's own source is released under the MIT License.

Orthonym uses two third-party Java programs. They are NOT part of this
repository and are NOT included in any Orthonym package. They are downloaded
from their official releases into the user's cache: `pip install` tries to
fetch them when it builds the package, a jar that is still missing is
downloaded the first time Orthonym needs it (unless ORTHONYM_NO_DOWNLOAD=1),
and `orthonym --fetch-jars` fetches them, or re-checks the ones in the jar
directory, at any time. Each download, and each jar found in the jar
directory, is checked against the SHA-256 below before it is used; a jar given
by ORTHONYM_OPSIN_JAR or ORTHONYM_CENTRES_JAR is used as given, without the
checksum check. Each jar keeps its own licence terms, listed here.

Orthonym runs them unmodified, as separate programs on the Java virtual
machine (by default in one in-process JVM via JPype, or as a `java -jar`
subprocess). If a jar cannot be found or downloaded, Orthonym stops with an
error rather than naming with less validation; setting ORTHONYM_ALLOW_REDUCED=1
opts in to naming without them. Without a working Java runtime it declines
every molecule rather than naming it unchecked.


OPSIN — opsin-cli-2.9.0-jar-with-dependencies.jar
-------------------------------------------------
- Component:  OPSIN (Open Parser for Systematic IUPAC Nomenclature), name -> structure
- Version:    2.9.0
- Download:   https://github.com/dan2097/opsin/releases/download/2.9.0/opsin-cli-2.9.0-jar-with-dependencies.jar
- SHA-256:    c2e29326c281f87b59a05d934d8589adac6e9d17b95b984931b3e739111b360f
- License:    MIT (OPSIN itself; Copyright (c) Daniel Lowe and contributors).
              The jar also bundles further third-party libraries under their own
              licences; see the OPSIN release and the notices inside the jar.
- Used for:   round-trip validation of names: OPSIN reads a name back to a
              structure, which must match the input. The few names the default
              tier ships that OPSIN cannot read in full (names from exact-match
              lists, a few name forms its grammar lacks or misreads, and names
              whose stereodescriptors it cannot parse) are marked by
              `orthonym --provenance`.
- Source:     https://github.com/dan2097/opsin


